# Bias - the medium, not the mix.
#
# Bias is the high-frequency signal a tape machine mixes into the record head,
# and setting it is exactly how you choose between a clean transfer and a
# compressed, rolled-off, saturated one. So a patch here is a **recording
# medium**: it colours what comes out and never the recordings, which makes it
# free to try, reversible, and comparable. Printing one is a separate act, and
# one that already exists - the freeze render.
#
# A patch deliberately carries none of the four lane levels or mutes. Those are
# the mix, and loading a medium must not wipe it; see MachineUi.patchKeeps.
#
# Init is the default here as in every bank, and it is the machine as built
# rather than a setting that undoes something: with every control at nought the
# whole chain is skipped, so a Bias track plays a file back sample for sample.
#
# Families: clean, tape, digital, broadcast, worn.

machine Bias

patch Init family=clean
  # Nothing at all: the recording, played back. Every value here is the machine
  # default, written out so the browser has something to come back to - and the
  # whole colour section is skipped rather than run at neutral, which is what
  # makes a stem export of a Bias track sample-identical to the file.
  hiss           0
  lowcut         20 Hz
  highcut        20000 Hz
  bump           0 dB
  sat            0
  comp           0
  wow            0
  flutter        0
  bleed          0
  drop           0
  bits           #20
  rate           1
  smear          0
  width          1

patch Warm family=clean
  # A transfer that was made carefully onto something good. The only thing
  # doing any work is the saturation, and it is working against the level the
  # signal actually reaches rather than against full scale.
  sat            0.35
  bump           2 dB
  bumpfreq       60 Hz
  highcut        18000 Hz

patch Wide family=clean
  # The low end a big transport gives you, and a little more image than was
  # printed. Nothing is lost here; it is the one patch that is a flattery.
  bump           3 dB
  bumpfreq       45 Hz
  sat            0.15
  width          1.3

patch Cassette family=tape
  # Type I ferric, played back on something ordinary: gone above thirteen and
  # a half, gone below forty-five, hiss you can hear in the gaps, and a
  # transport that never quite holds still.
  hiss           0.35
  hisstone       0.55
  lowcut         45 Hz
  highcut        13500 Hz
  bump           3 dB
  bumpfreq       95 Hz
  sat            0.4
  comp           0.3
  wow            0.25
  flutter        0.3
  width          0.95

patch Portastudio family=tape
  # The Portastudio: four tracks across a strip of tape meant for two, so the
  # tracks are narrower, the hiss is worse, and **what is on one is faintly on
  # the next**. That last is `bleed`, and it is the reason muting a track on
  # one of these never quite silences it.
  hiss           0.5
  hisstone       0.6
  lowcut         60 Hz
  highcut        11000 Hz
  bump           4 dB
  bumpfreq       110 Hz
  sat            0.5
  comp           0.55
  wow            0.3
  flutter        0.4
  bleed          0.45
  width          0.9

patch Reel family=tape
  # Fifteen inches a second on quarter inch: wide, quiet, and with enough
  # headroom that the saturation is something you go looking for rather than
  # something that happens to you. The head bump sits low, where a big
  # transport puts it.
  hiss           0.12
  hisstone       0.35
  lowcut         25 Hz
  highcut        18500 Hz
  bump           2.5 dB
  bumpfreq       42 Hz
  sat            0.28
  comp           0.15
  wow            0.1
  flutter        0.08
  speed          0.6

patch Cartridge family=tape
  # The cartridge: an endless loop dragged past the head by a pinch roller
  # that has been doing it since 1971. Duller than a cassette, wowier than
  # anything, and with the odd moment where the tape lifts away.
  hiss           0.42
  hisstone       0.45
  lowcut         70 Hz
  highcut        9000 Hz
  bump           3.5 dB
  bumpfreq       105 Hz
  sat            0.45
  comp           0.4
  wow            0.5
  flutter        0.35
  bleed          0.3
  drop           0.15

patch Micro family=tape
  # A dictaphone. Three hundred to four thousand, squashed flat, mono, and
  # running at whatever speed the batteries feel like. For a voice memo that
  # became a song.
  hiss           0.7
  hisstone       0.75
  lowcut         320 Hz
  highcut        4200 Hz
  sat            0.6
  comp           0.8
  wow            0.55
  flutter        0.6
  speed          1.8
  drop           0.25
  width          0

patch MiniDisc family=digital
  # **No hiss at all**, which is the point of standing it next to the tapes:
  # its character is entirely codec. A band edge just under sixteen, a smear
  # that thickens transients instead of sharpening them, and an image the
  # coder narrowed to save bits.
  lowcut         20 Hz
  highcut        15800 Hz
  bits           #12
  smear          0.35
  width          0.8

patch DAT family=digital
  # Sixteen bits and a brick wall, and almost nothing else. It is nearly
  # transparent and that is the whole of it - the one patch here that sounds
  # like the nineties by not sounding like anything.
  highcut        19500 Hz
  bits           #12
  sat            0.05

patch Sampler family=digital
  # Twelve bits at thirty kilohertz: the other digital character, where the
  # quantisation and the aliasing are the instrument rather than the price.
  highcut        15000 Hz
  bits           #8
  rate           0.62
  sat            0.2

patch Phone family=broadcast
  # Three hundred to thirty-four hundred, eight bits, and a compressor that
  # never lets go. A line, not a room.
  lowcut         300 Hz
  highcut        3400 Hz
  bits           #4
  comp           0.6
  sat            0.3
  width          0

patch Shortwave family=broadcast
  # A signal that has come a long way and is not sure it will arrive: narrow,
  # noisy, fading in and out, and drifting on a transport that is the
  # ionosphere.
  lowcut         260 Hz
  highcut        3000 Hz
  hiss           0.55
  hisstone       0.6
  drop           0.4
  wow            0.35
  speed          2.2
  comp           0.5
  width          0

patch AM family=broadcast
  # Wider than a telephone and squashed harder, which is what a station does
  # to be loud on a small speaker.
  lowcut         120 Hz
  highcut        5200 Hz
  hiss           0.3
  hisstone       0.45
  comp           0.55
  sat            0.25
  width          0

patch Shellac family=worn
  # A 78: two hundred to three thousand, surface noise under everything, and
  # a turntable with a story. The wow is slow because the disc is big.
  lowcut         220 Hz
  highcut        3200 Hz
  hiss           0.6
  hisstone       0.3
  drop           0.55
  wow            0.6
  speed          0.5
  sat            0.35
  comp           0.35
  width          0

patch Wire family=worn
  # Steel wire dragged past a head at six feet a second, by a machine that
  # cannot keep it straight. Hissier than tape and far less stable.
  lowcut         260 Hz
  highcut        4800 Hz
  hiss           0.65
  hisstone       0.5
  sat            0.4
  wow            0.45
  flutter        0.5
  speed          1.4
  drop           0.3
  width          0

patch Wax family=worn
  # A cylinder. The narrowest thing here and the least steady: what a hundred
  # and twenty years does to a recording, without the hundred and twenty years.
  lowcut         350 Hz
  highcut        2600 Hz
  hiss           0.5
  hisstone       0.25
  drop           0.4
  wow            0.75
  speed          0.35
  sat            0.45
  comp           0.4
  width          0
